# A program that does not finish is killed after --timeout seconds and
# reported as '[timeout]' (plan M0.b: every child process runs under a
# wall-clock limit, because a JIT can hang on bad IL). The busy loop gives up
# by itself after 120 s, so the outer 60 s bound also catches a harness that
# waits for the program instead of killing it.

# RUN: %ilasm -dll -output=%t.dll %S/Inputs/spin.il
# RUN: %{harness_check} within 60 %msil_exec --timeout 5 --expect %S/Inputs/spin --out %t.out %t.dll
# RUN: %{harness_check} equals %t.out "[timeout]"
